Reproducible (ordered) assembly and a locked, counted hand-over in the multi-threaded factorization of SPOOLES 2.2 (SPOOLES is in the public domain; this is a
change of its source made by FEA Studio, dates of the change: 21. 11. 2026). Applied by installer/windows/build_ccx.sh and installer/linux/build_ccx.sh with
`patch -p1` in the folder of the unpacked spooles.2.2.tgz (SPOOLES.2.2/), after the correction of util.c and before SPOOLES is compiled. The same file lies in
installer/windows/patches/ and installer/linux/patches/. The comments "FEA Studio change, 21. 11. 2026" in the hunks are the notice of the change. The text
above the first hunk header is ignored by patch(1).

Why 1 (ordered assembly): with several equation-solver threads SPOOLES sums the pieces of a front in the order in which they ARRIVE: the aggregates that other
threads hand to the owner of the front (ChvList is a list in the order of arrival, and the owner took it as soon as something was in it) and the internal updates
(a list that FrontMtx_update reverses each time and from which it takes the finished sources in any order). Two runs therefore differ in the last digits (200
factorizations of the same matrix gave 188 to 200 different solutions, bit for bit), and an eigenvalue analysis of a degenerate cluster (BK3) or a comparison at
1e-9 (V12 (c)) can differ from run to run without an error. The change makes the order of every sum independent of the timing, for a given number of threads:
- Chv gets the field `sender` (the thread that made the aggregate); the sender sets it before it hands the chevron over.
- the owner of a front assembles the aggregates only when all its internal updates are done AND all aggregates are in the list (no early partial assembly), in
  the ascending order of `sender` (a stable insertion sort of the short list).
- FrontMtx_factorSetup reverses the update lists of a threaded factorization (ascending source fronts) and the new FrontMtx_updateOrdered takes the updates
  strictly in the order of the list (it stops at the first source that is not finished and keeps the rest of the list); the serial factorization
  (FrontMtx_update) is untouched, so a one-thread run is bit for bit what it was.
Measured (the standalone driver on the matrices dumped from ccx, 3 to 16 threads, also with random delays at the protocol points of the lists): 1 distinct
solution in 20 ... 300 factorizations (Y1, Y15, Y9, CY2 blocks, BK3, KKT matrices with 682 / 4 673 postponed pivots, synthetic) against 188 ... 300 with the
original code; no change of the time of a factorization (Y15, 4 threads, two cores: 14.1 s against 14.1 s for 60 factorizations). The early take of single
aggregates of the original code (a list read and emptied while other threads still add to it, with the chevrons recycled at once) does not exist any more; it is
one candidate for the still unknown mechanism of the lost updates of the first F17 candidate (see patches/ccx_2.23_spooles_serial_solve.patch), which the check
of the factors of that patch catches whatever the cause.

Why 2 (locked hand-over, review hardening): the original ChvList leaves a list without the mutex when it has only one source thread (flags 'N') and reads the
head and the count of any list without the mutex; this is correct under the memory order of x86 when the compiler keeps the order of the two stores, which was
checked in the object code of both toolchains, but it is the kind of protocol that fails silently elsewhere. The change: FrontMtx_aggregateList and
FrontMtx_postList pass no flags[] (every list of a threaded factorization is locked), and ChvList_isListNonempty, ChvList_isCountZero and ChvList_getList read
and clear under the same mutex. A list in the counting of the aggregates is short (232 aggregates in 79 fronts of a 20 000 equation model), so the mutex costs
nothing measurable.

Why 3 (conservation check): ChvList counts the chevrons that were added (nadded), taken by ChvList_getList (ntaken) and reported as assembled by the owner
(nassembled, ChvList_noteAssembled in assembleAggregates). At the end of FrontMtx_MT_factorPencil, after all threads are joined and before the lists are freed,
ChvList_closeCheck verifies that every list is empty, every count is zero and nadded = ntaken = nassembled; a violation prints a warning and sets the error
flag to -2, which the CalculiX side (spooles.c fea_judge) treats like a failed check of the factors (repeat, then one thread).

